// `switch` with exhaustiveness checking (ADR-0067) — the first feature of **W4.5**, and the wave that
// moved *before* W4 because checking showed it did not need comptime.
//
// PLAN §2.1 placed W4.5 after W4 "because exhaustiveness diagnostics want comptime type info". That is
// a want, not a need: exhaustiveness over an enum needs only the member set, and `Pool::enum_members` is
// already populated during checking (ADR-0041 §4). Both prerequisites were verified by *running* —
// `c == .GREEN` and `c == Colour.GREEN` already worked — so the wave came forward. ADR-0067 §0 records
// the amendment, because a wave order justified by a dependency that does not exist is the "plans that
// contradict themselves" failure mode PLAN §5 names.
//
// Six things here are worth checking rather than assuming, and the `exit` at the end gives each teeth
// under the differential harness — a wrong arm, a fallthrough, or a scrutinee evaluated twice would each
// be a different exit status:
//
//   * a **bare `.RED`** works as a case (ADR-0041 §2 step 5, the deferral this settles), because the
//     scrutinee's type is the expected type the arm's value resolves against — the same mechanism
//     `c == .GREEN` already used;
//   * a **qualified `Colour.RED`** is equally legal, and the two spellings name one member;
//   * **no fallthrough** (ADR-0067 §5): an arm runs and the `switch` ends, so `describe` returns one
//     value rather than falling into the next arm's;
//   * an **`else` arm** is the catch-all and makes a non-enum `switch` total (§4) — `classify` switches
//     on an `s64`, which has no finite member set to be exhaustive over (§3);
//   * the **scrutinee is evaluated once**, before any comparison (§6), which matters because a
//     `switch f() { … }` must not call `f` per arm — `from_call` switches on a call's result;
//   * both engines agree, because a `switch` lowers to the branch chain an `if`/`else if` over the same
//     comparisons already produces — **no new MIR node** and no back-end change.
#import "Basic";

/// Auto-numbered 0, 1, 2 — the same enum `032-enums.jr` uses, so the two files cannot disagree about
/// what an enum is.
Colour :: enum {
    RED;
    GREEN;
    BLUE;
}

/// Exhaustive over `Colour` with **bare** members, and no `else` — which is only legal because every
/// member is named. Omitting one is E0258 (`type-errors/059`), and adding an `else` here would be E0260,
/// since it could never run.
describe :: (c: Colour) -> s64 {
    r := 0;
    switch c {
        case .RED;
            r = 1;
        case .GREEN;
            r = 2;
        case .BLUE;
            r = 4;
    }
    return r;
}

/// The same match written with **qualified** members, proving the two spellings are interchangeable.
/// A reader comparing this with `describe` should see only the spelling change.
describe_qualified :: (c: Colour) -> s64 {
    r := 0;
    switch c {
        case Colour.RED;
            r = 1;
        case Colour.GREEN;
            r = 2;
        case Colour.BLUE;
            r = 4;
    }
    return r;
}

/// A `switch` on an `s64`, made total by `else`. Exhaustiveness is **not** checked here (ADR-0067 §3):
/// an integer has no finite member set, so the check would be approximate rather than true, and an
/// `else` is what makes the match total instead.
classify :: (n: s64) -> s64 {
    r := 0;
    switch n {
        case 1;
            r = 10;
        case 2;
            r = 20;
        else;
            r = 30;
    }
    return r;
}

/// Returns a value for the scrutinee, so `from_call` matches on a **call's result**. If the lowering
/// evaluated the scrutinee per arm, this would be called once per comparison.
two :: () -> s64 {
    return 2;
}

/// Switches on a call, which is the shape that makes "evaluated once" observable in the MIR: the
/// snapshot shows one `call` before the first comparison, not one per arm (ADR-0067 §6).
from_call :: () -> s64 {
    r := 0;
    switch two() {
        case 1;
            r = 100;
        case 2;
            r = 200;
        else;
            r = 300;
    }
    return r;
}

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    // Each member reaches its own arm, and only its own — no fallthrough.
    if describe(Colour.RED) == 1 {
        n = n + 1;
    }
    if describe(Colour.GREEN) == 2 {
        n = n + 2;
    }
    if describe(Colour.BLUE) == 4 {
        n = n + 4;
    }

    // The qualified spelling agrees with the bare one.
    if describe_qualified(Colour.GREEN) == 2 {
        n = n + 8;
    }

    // A matched case and the `else` fallback, on a non-enum scrutinee.
    if classify(2) == 20 {
        n = n + 16;
    }
    if classify(99) == 30 {
        n = n + 32;
    }

    // A call as the scrutinee.
    if from_call() == 200 {
        n = n + 64;
    }

    // Every assertion: 127.
    if n == 127 {
        exit(0);
    }
    exit(1);
}
